---

<BaseLayout
  title="Pest Control Seagraves TX | Scorpions &amp; Termites"
  description="Scorpion, termite, rodent and weed work in Seagraves, sixteen miles from our Seminole base. Licensed TPCL 0918482, no contracts, no trip fee."
  path="/locations/seagraves/"
  quickAnswer={quickAnswer}
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
  faqs={faqs}
  service={{
    name: 'Pest Control in Seagraves, Texas',
    description: 'Scorpion, spider, termite, rodent and general pest control for homes, manufactured homes, acreage and businesses in Seagraves and northern Gaines County.',
    areaServed: ['Gaines County'],
  }}
>
  <Crumbs items={crumbs} />

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Gaines County · about 16 miles from Seminole</p>
      <h1>Pest Control in Seagraves, Texas</h1>
      <div class="measure"><AnswerBox text={quickAnswer} /></div>
      <Trust />
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          Seagraves is the closest town on this route to our base, and the same county. That is not a
          marketing line — it changes what is actually possible. Sixteen miles is a twenty-minute drive.
          It means a call in the morning can become a visit in the afternoon, it means a callback under
          the guarantee is not a scheduling negotiation, and it means the follow-up visit that termite
          and rodent work depends on actually happens on time.
        </p>
        <p>
          It also means the ground is familiar. Same county, same soil, same fields, same weather, same
          ordinance framework running out of the same county courthouse. Nothing about a Seagraves
          property is a surprise.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Seven days — the shortest notice on the route</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          Every town out here enforces a maximum vegetation height, and people move between these towns
          constantly assuming the rule travels with them. It does not, and Seagraves is the one where
          getting it wrong costs you the most time.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The limit is twelve inches.</strong> Article 7.02 of the Seagraves Code of Ordinances
          makes it unlawful to fail to keep property free of weeds, grass and undergrowth over twelve
          inches tall. The same section sweeps in household trash, garbage, used building materials,
          discarded fixtures and appliances, stagnant water, dead animals, brush piles, debris and
          fire-hazard material — all of it grouped as objectionable material.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The duty does not stop at your property line.</strong> Section 7.02.001(b) extends it
          out to the curb line or the edge of the pavement, and to the centre of the adjacent alley. The
          strip most people think of as the city's is yours to keep, and alleys are where this catches
          people most often, because nobody looks at them.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>You get seven days.</strong> Not ten, as in Denver City and Andrews. Seven. Notice is
          given personally in writing, or by certified mail with return receipt, and if personal service
          fails the city can publish in the newspaper and post on the property. After seven days the city
          may do the work, pay for it, charge you the expense and file a lien statement.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Above forty-eight inches there is no notice at all.</strong> Section 7.02.005 permits
          immediate abatement of weeds higher than four feet where they are an immediate danger to health,
          life or safety. You are notified within ten days afterwards and can request a hearing within
          thirty. That is a very different thing from a warning letter.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>And one notice can cover a year.</strong> Under section 7.02.003, if you commit another
          violation of the same kind on or before the first anniversary of a notice, the city may correct
          it without further notice and assess the expense against the property, provided it has not been
          told the ownership changed. This is the single most commonly misunderstood provision in West
          Texas weed enforcement. People assume every abatement comes with its own warning. For twelve
          months after the first one, it does not have to.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The lien has teeth.</strong> Section 7.02.006 puts interest at ten percent per annum
          from the date the city pays, files the statement with the county clerk, makes the lien inferior
          only to tax liens and liens for street improvements, permits foreclosure, and makes the filed
          statement prima facie proof of the expenses.
        </p>
        <p>
          One more detail worth knowing: section 7.02.008 says the city may, but is not required to, send
          a courtesy notice before the formal one. If you have had a courtesy notice in the past and are
          expecting another, that expectation is not written into the ordinance.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>One in five homes here is manufactured</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          American Community Survey figures put Seagraves at about 1,077 housing units, of which roughly
          211 — near enough one in five — are mobile or manufactured homes. That is a materially higher
          share than the towns further south on this route, and it changes the work.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The underside is the perimeter.</strong> On a slab house the treatment band runs around
          the foundation. On a manufactured home there is an accessible void beneath the floor, enclosed
          by skirting that was fitted years ago and has usually shifted since. The gap between the
          skirting and the ground is the single most productive entry point on the property, and it is
          almost never the thing people point at when they call.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Penetrations come up through the floor.</strong> Water, waste and electrical enter from
          below rather than through a wall. Those openings are frequently oversized and rarely sealed
          properly, and they put a route from the void directly into a cabinet or a closet. A mouse in the
          kitchen of a manufactured home is very often a mouse that never went outside.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Moisture collects where you cannot see it.</strong> The void under a manufactured home
          is shaded, sheltered and slower to dry than open ground. In a region this dry that makes it a
          destination. A leaking supply line or a condensate discharge under there will support a
          population year-round that no perimeter spray reaches.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Scorpions do very well in it.</strong> Dark, undisturbed, cool relative to the ground
          outside, and connected to the interior by every one of those floor penetrations. Scorpion work
          on a manufactured home is mostly about that void and the skirting, and comparatively little
          about the walls you are looking at.
        </p>
        <p>
          None of that makes a manufactured home harder to keep clear. It makes it a different job, and
          the companies that quote it as though it were a small slab house are the ones you end up calling
          twice.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>A town built in 1969 and rebuilt in 1928</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          Two dates explain most of the structural pest picture in Seagraves.
        </p>
        <p>
          The median year of construction for housing here is <strong>1969</strong>. That is a stock now
          well past fifty years old, built before modern sealing practice, and it shows up in the same
          handful of places on almost every property: door thresholds that no longer seat, weatherstripping
          that has hardened and cracked in fifty summers of this sun, utility penetrations cut and never
          sealed, weep holes without screens, and garage door bottoms that have compressed.
        </p>
        <p>
          None of those are defects in the sense of anything being wrong with the house. They are what
          fifty years of West Texas wind, dust and heat does to seals. But they are entry points, and on
          a house of this vintage the exclusion work is usually worth more than the treatment.
        </p>
        <p>
          The other date is <strong>1928</strong>, when a fire destroyed much of the Seagraves business
          district. What went back up went up in brick, which is why the commercial core reads the way it
          does. Masonry commercial buildings of that era have their own pest profile — deep wall cavities,
          historic penetrations from utilities that have been rerouted twice since, and gaps at the
          junction between the original structure and everything added later.
        </p>
        <p>
          It is worth remembering that Seagraves was the largest town in Gaines County until around 1950,
          when Seminole passed it. The building stock reflects a town that was substantial earlier than
          its current population suggests, and older commercial property carries older problems.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Playa ground, and a soil series named after the town</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          There is a genuine curiosity here that turns out to be useful. The United States Department of
          Agriculture maintains an official soil series called <strong>Seagraves</strong> — sandy eolian
          deposits over loamy lacustrine material, occurring on the nearly level floors of playa basins
          across the Southern High Plains, moderately well drained, slowly permeable, and ponding briefly
          in some years.
        </p>
        <p>
          The series is named for the town rather than typified in Gaines County, so it would be wrong to
          say that this is the soil under Seagraves. What it describes accurately is the kind of ground
          that characterises this part of the plains, and the phrase that matters for pest work is
          <em>ponds briefly in some years</em>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Gaines County drains to scattered playas and draws rather than to any river. There are no
          permanent streams. What that means practically is that surface water out here is episodic: dry
          for long stretches, then holding for days or weeks after a real rain, then dry again. Mosquito
          pressure follows that pattern rather than running continuously, and the useful thing to know is
          that the count climbs roughly a week to ten days after a significant rain and falls away again.
          Treating on a fixed calendar misses it; treating in response to the weather does not.
        </p>
        <p>
          The county soils themselves are sandy loam and sand over a red clay subsoil, supporting mesquite,
          shinnery and catclaw. Sand matters for two reasons. It drains fast, which means moisture
          concentrates wherever something artificial holds it — an irrigation head, a downspout, a
          condensate line — and those concentration points become the whole story on a property. And it
          moves, which is why a termite barrier established in sand is a different proposition from one in
          heavy clay, and why grade changes around a foundation deserve more attention here than they get.
        </p>
        <p>
          Elevation is about 3,353 feet. Cedar Lake, the largest salt lake on the Texas plains, sits in
          north-eastern Gaines County. Annual rainfall runs around sixteen inches with a growing season a
          little over two hundred days.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>What we get called about in Seagraves</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          <strong>Scorpions, from June through September.</strong> Striped bark scorpions, and they are
          the defining residential pest of this whole region. AgriLife entomologists note that scorpions
          move indoors when it gets excessively dry outside, which makes a long dry June a reliable
          predictor of a bad July. They are nocturnal, they climb well, and they turn up in attics far
          more often than people expect. The work is harbourage and exclusion far more than spraying.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Field-driven rodents, especially after harvest.</strong> Seagraves sits in cotton,
          peanut, sorghum and grain country — Gaines County is among the leading cotton producers in
          Texas, with something like four hundred thousand irrigated acres in the county. When a field
          comes off, the population that was living in it goes somewhere, and the somewhere is whatever
          structures are nearest. Properties on the edge of town see this as a sudden event rather than a
          gradual increase, at roughly the same point every year.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Ants, in several forms that get confused with each other.</strong> Harvester ants
          outdoors with their bare circular mounds and foraging trails, ordinary nuisance ants indoors on
          a moisture trail, and occasionally something that needs identifying before it is treated because
          the wrong product on the wrong ant scatters a colony rather than eliminating it.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Spiders, including black widows and brown recluse.</strong> Both are present in this
          region and both are worth taking seriously without panic. AgriLife notes brown recluse are found
          throughout Texas, sheltering in firewood stacks, stored boxes in attics and low-traffic
          crevices, and that bites are rare and accidental. The treatment is the harbourage — the stacked
          material — rather than the web you can see.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Termites, which most people here have been told they do not have.</strong> The
          assumption that West Texas is too dry for termites is wrong and it is the reason activity so
          often goes unnoticed until it is substantial. Subterranean termites work below grade where the
          moisture is, and on a slab they can be into a wall for a long time before anything is visible
          from inside.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Weeds and lot condition, on a seven-day clock.</strong> More of this work than you might
          expect is people who have received a notice, or who own a lot in town and live somewhere else.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>If you own property in Seagraves and live elsewhere</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          A meaningful share of the residential property in a town this size is owned by people who are
          not standing in front of it. Roughly forty percent of occupied housing here is rented, and
          beyond that there are vacant lots, inherited houses and property held by people who moved to
          Lubbock or Midland or out of state years ago.
        </p>
        <p>
          The ordinance mechanics above are aimed squarely at you, and they are less forgiving at a
          distance. Notice can be served by certified mail to the address of record — which is only useful
          if that address is current with the appraisal district. Where personal service fails, the city
          can publish in the newspaper and post a notice on the property itself, and neither of those
          reaches somebody living three hundred miles away. The seven days run regardless.
        </p>
        <p>
          Then the one-year rule compounds it. The first notice you may never have seen starts a twelve
          month period in which further abatement needs no notice at all, and the first you hear of any of
          it can be a lien statement filed with the county clerk accruing ten percent.
        </p>
        <p>
          The practical answer is a standing arrangement rather than a reaction. A lot kept on a schedule
          through the growing season costs a fraction of an abatement plus interest, and it removes the
          entire enforcement question from your life. If you own several properties here, they can be run
          on one visit and one invoice.
        </p>
        <p>
          If a notice has already arrived, read us the date printed on it when you call. In Seagraves that
          date is worth more than anything else you can tell us.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Services in Seagraves</h2>
      <div class="grid c3" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
        {SERVICES.filter((s) => s.priority <= 2).map((s) => (
          <a class="card" href={`/services/${s.slug}/`}>
            <h3>{s.name}</h3><p>{s.summary}</p>
          </a>
        ))}
      </div>
      <div class="measure" style="margin-top:2rem">
        <p>
          <strong>No contracts.</strong> Recurring service runs bi-monthly or quarterly, priced on the
          size of the property, the pressure and the frequency, with a higher initial visit. Termite, bed
          bug, mosquito, lawn, brush and junk work is individually quoted after an inspection.
        </p>
        <p>
          Covered by the <a href="/our-guarantee/">30-day service guarantee</a>, with termite work
          carrying its own one-year re-treatment warranty. Military discount. Cash, check, card in person
          or online, Venmo, CashApp or Zelle, and financing available on larger jobs. Se habla español.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Why sixteen miles is the whole argument</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          No pest control company is listed at a Seagraves street address. Directory searches return more
          than thirty companies serving the town, and their addresses are in Lubbock, Odessa, Amarillo,
          Abilene, Shallowater, Brownfield, Slaton — and Seminole. Whoever you call is driving in.
        </p>
        <p>
          That reframes the question. It is not who is local, because nobody is. It is who is closest, and
          what closeness actually buys you.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>It buys response.</strong> A company running Seagraves from Lubbock is covering
          seventy-odd miles and will reasonably want to batch the trip. From Seminole it is sixteen, and
          a same-day call is a normal request rather than a favour.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>It buys follow-up.</strong> This is where distance really shows. The thirty-day
          guarantee and the interim visits on a recurring plan are worth exactly as much as somebody's
          willingness to make the drive for them. A callback that costs an operator half a day tends to
          become a scheduling problem. A callback that costs twenty minutes does not.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>It buys the follow-up visits the work actually needs.</strong> Termite and rodent jobs
          are not single-visit work. They involve returning at a specific interval to check what happened,
          and the interval is not negotiable with the biology. Distance is the most common reason those
          return visits slip.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>And it buys knowing the ground.</strong> Same county, same soil, same fields, same
          ordinance, same weather. The person quoting your property grew up eighteen minutes down the
          road. That is not a substitute for competence, but combined with it, it means the inspection
          starts from a real understanding of what a property in this town is likely to be doing.
        </p>
        <p>
          None of that makes an Odessa or Lubbock company a bad choice. It does mean the question worth
          asking any of them is how often they are out this way anyway.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Faq items={faqs} heading="Seagraves pest control questions" />
  <Cta
    market="Seagraves"
    line="Sixteen miles up the road, same county. Tell him what you are seeing and whether you are working to a deadline — and if a city notice has arrived, read him the date on it."
  />
</BaseLayout>
